course: dictionary
lesson: 5209
title: Jargon: special words for special groups
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Jargon is the special language a group uses among themselves. Doctors, lawyers and computer experts all have words that make sense to them but are hard for everyone else. «Adverse event» is a doctor's way of saying harm caused by treatment.
explain: Jargon can also be confusing or pompous language, the kind that makes a simple idea sound grand. «As previously mentioned» is office jargon for «I already told you this». Plain English says the same thing in fewer, clearer words.
explain: You can use context to work out jargon, just as you would any new word. «Harvested» is hunting jargon, so the sentence around it tells you it is about hunters. When you can, swap in a plain word and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Jargon is special words used by a particular group, such as doctors, lawyers or computer experts.
fact: Jargon can also mean confusing or pompous language that is hard to understand.
fact: «Adverse event» is medical jargon for harm or injury caused by medical treatment.
fact: «Per my last email» is office jargon meaning you did not read my email properly.
fact: In maths, «numerator» and «denominator» are maths jargon: the numerator is the top number of a fraction and the denominator is the bottom number.
exercise: «Adverse event» is medical jargon. What does it mean? | A happy day at the hospital | A new kind of bandage | *Harm or injury caused by medical treatment | A visit from a friendly nurse
exercise: What is jargon? | Words that everyone in every group knows | Words that are always spelled wrong | Words written only in poems | *Special words used by a particular group that are hard for others to understand
exercise: «As previously mentioned» is office jargon. What does it mean in plain English? | *I already told you this | Thank you very much | Hello, how are you | Please read the book
test: Which word is maths jargon for the top number of a fraction? | *Numerator | Denominator | Decimal | Equation
test: «Per my last email» is office jargon. What does it mean? | Please write back soon | I have sent you a present | Thank you for your email | *You did not read my email properly
exam: Which best describes jargon-free writing? | Written in a secret code | Full of special words only experts know | Confusing and pompous | *Clear, plain words that anyone can understand
